load(":cases.bzl", "adopt_system_libs_case")

# The pinned system libraries of a package adopted by `mojo_adopted_library` reach the test runs of an old-rule dependent (tools/build/mojo/system_libs.bzl).
# `:codec_lib` stands in for the gated `:lib` of a `mojo_package` that pins libsqlite and libzlib: it exports their directories. `:codec` opens libsqlite3.so.0 by its
# bare name, which a farm worker does not have. Every target below must BUILD (they are ordinary targets, built by a plain `buck2 build`):
#   :consumer                       an old-rule library whose welded test calls the codec
#   :consumer[coverage]             its kcov run, and :consumer[coverage][branch] its branch run (`coverage_debug` gives it both whatever the switch says)
#   :consumer2                      a library two levels below the adopted label (it depends on :consumer): its welded test calls the codec
#   :program_test                   a `mojo_test`: `buck2 test` runs it through the gate
#   :probe                          a `mojo_shared_lib` whose driver loads it and calls an export that opens SQLite
#
# `-c adopt_system_libs.case=unpinned` adds the same targets with the suffix `_unpinned` over a `:lib` that forgot its pins. `:consumer_unpinned` and
# `:program_test_unpinned` must FAIL, in the action of the welded test, because the loader cannot open libsqlite3.so.0. The case also adds `:via_test_deps` (lists the
# adopted label in `test_deps` only: green, its own test is given the libraries) and `:leak_check` (depends on it and on the plain codec: must FAIL the same way, because
# the `test_deps` directories do not reach a dependent). The scenario is
# tools/build/selftest:planted_red_builds_adopt_system_libs.
adopt_system_libs_case()
